"This surah contains rulings strictly ordained by Allah and clear verses. It delineates the legal framework and fundamental duties of Islamic civilization. First, it addresses issues of chastity, honor, and family law.\n\nIt is commanded that the woman and man guilty of unlawful sexual intercourse (zina) be flogged with a hundred stripes. Here, a distinction is made between 'zaniya' (an active female fornicator) and 'mazniyya' (a woman subjected to unlawful intercourse); a woman forced into unlawful intercourse is not subject to the hadd penalty. The manner of striking: the stick should be as thick as a finger, it should not be struck against a bare body, and vulnerable organs must be avoided. The punishment must be carried out in the presence of a group; this group should consist of at least four people. This both ensures justice and serves as an admonition.\n\nA man or woman guilty of zina may only marry someone who is likewise guilty of zina or a polytheist (mushrik). This is forbidden to the believers. The occasion of revelation of the verse was the brothels in Medina. Some scholars state that this ruling applied to a specific situation, while others maintain it is general. Companions such as Aisha and Ibn Mas'ud maintained that marrying those guilty of zina is forbidden, while others stated that it is permissible.\n\nThose who slander chaste women with accusations of zina and fail to produce four witnesses are to be flogged with eighty stripes, and their testimony is never to be accepted again. However, those who repent and reform themselves are excluded from this ruling. In the Hanafi school, repentance does not drop the hadd penalty; only the sin concerning the right of Allah is forgiven; repentance is not sufficient for their testimony to be accepted.\n\nA man who accuses his wife of zina and has no other witnesses swears four times in the name of Allah, and on the fifth invokes the curse of Allah upon himself if he is lying. The woman likewise saves herself from punishment by taking similar oaths. This is the process of lian (mutual imprecation). The verse reminds what the condition would have been were it not for Allah's grace and mercy.\n\nThe requirement of four witnesses is stipulated for the crime of zina. However, if a person catches his own wife in the act of zina, finding four witnesses is almost impossible. In this situation, lian (mutual imprecation) was legislated. According to narration, Sa'd ibn Ubadah and Asim ibn Adiyy requested clarification on this matter; subsequently, Hilal ibn Umayyah reported that he had seen his wife with another man, whereupon revelation came to the Messenger of Allah and the verses of lian were revealed.\n\nAccording to the verses of lian (An-Nur 24:6-10), when a husband accuses his wife of zina and has no four witnesses, he swears four times by Allah that he is speaking the truth. On the fifth, he invokes the curse of Allah upon himself if he is lying. The woman defends herself in the same manner by swearing four times, and on the fifth invoking the wrath of Allah upon herself if he is telling the truth. When lian is completed in this manner, the husband is spared the penalty of slander (qadhf), the woman is spared the punishment for zina, and a separation takes place between them.\n\nThe Incident of the Slander (Ifk) befell Aisha. On the return from an expedition, she lost her necklace, became separated from the army while searching for it, and Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal found her, helped her mount his camel, and brought her to the army. Thereupon, the hypocrites and some gullible individuals spread slander about Aisha. Aisha remained in grief for a month until Allah Almighty revealed the verses exonerating her.\n\nIn the verses of the Ifk (An-Nur 24:11-20), important lessons are imparted to the believers: upon hearing the slander, they should immediately have thought well of their own people and said, 'This is a clear slander.' Because they could not produce four witnesses, the slanderers were deemed liars. For each of those who spread this slander, there is punishment in proportion to their sin, while for the ringleader, Abdullah ibn Ubayy, there is a tremendous punishment. In this world as well, the hadd penalty was inflicted upon those who spread this slander.\n\nIn the continuation of the verses (An-Nur 24:21-25), people are warned against following the footsteps of Satan, and those of virtue are commanded not to swear off giving help to the poor. Those who slander chaste, believing women are cursed in this world and the Hereafter, and it is proclaimed that a great punishment awaits them. On that Day, their tongues, hands, and feet will testify against them.\n\nIn An-Nur 24:26, it is stated that 'vile things are for vile people, and good things are for good people'—that is, bad women are for bad men, and pure women are for pure men. The pure family of the Prophet (peace be upon him) is free from what the slanderers say. For them, there is forgiveness and a noble provision.\n\nIn the subsequent verses (An-Nur 24:27-31), seeking permission and extending greetings of peace when entering homes are commanded. Believing men are commanded to lower their gaze from what is unlawful and guard their chastity; believing women are likewise commanded to lower their gaze, not display their adornment, draw their veils over their bosoms, not display their adornment to anyone other than their mahrams (unmarriageable kin), and not strike their feet on the ground to draw attention to their hidden adornments. These rulings are aimed at preserving chastity and privacy in society.\n\nThe verses command believers to ask permission when entering others' homes and to make their presence known to the occupants. Isti'nas is notifying the household through sounds such as coughing or pronouncing the takbir. Asking for permission is done three times: at the first, attention is drawn; at the second, preparations are made; and at the third, permission is either granted or denied. If no answer is received after the third time, one must leave. Banging loudly on the door or shouting is unlawful.\n\nPlaces that are uninhabited but in which a person has a right of use (such as shops, inns, or public baths) may be entered without permission. This ruling was revealed upon Abu Bakr's inquiry regarding entering inns for trade. However, if such places are rented, the ruling of the preceding verses applies.\n\nBelieving men are commanded to lower their gaze from the unlawful and to guard their private parts. Guarding the private parts (furuj) also includes covering the awrah (intimate parts). For men, the awrah extends from below the navel to the knees; Allah is fully aware of all that they do.\n\nBelieving women are also commanded to lower their gaze, guard their private parts, and not reveal their adornment. Adornment includes both jewelry and the attractive parts of the body. Necessarily exposed parts, such as the face and hands, are exempt. The head covering must be drawn over the collars, concealing the hair, neck, and bosom. This command of covering does not apply in the presence of mahrams—such as a husband, father, son, brother, paternal uncle, maternal uncle, father-in-law, or stepson—nor before elderly male attendants or children who have not yet reached puberty. Different rulings apply to female slaves.\n\nWomen should not strike their feet on the ground as they walk so that their hidden ornaments may be heard. The primary responsibility for societal corruption lies with men; therefore, both men and women must repent and turn to Allah.\n\nArranging marriage for the single and for slaves is encouraged. Marriage should not be avoided out of fear of poverty; Allah grants provision to whom He wills. Those who cannot find the means to marry must maintain their chastity. Entering into mukataba (a contract of emancipation) with slaves is recommended, and compelling female slaves who desire to remain chaste into prostitution is forbidden. For female slaves who are coerced, Allah is Forgiving and Merciful.\n\nThese verses contain clear proofs, lessons from previous nations, and admonitions for the God-fearing.\n\nVerse 34 connects the preceding part of the surah with what follows by recalling matters such as the rulings revealed up to this point, the exemplary story of the Ifk, and the lights of deliverance for the God-fearing (muttaqin). It also states that this story was not the first of its kind, that great figures such as Joseph and Mary were likewise subjected to similar slanders, and that this was not an evil for them, but rather a good.\n\nIn verse 35, Allah is described as the light of the heavens and the earth. This parable is like a niche in which is a lamp; the lamp is within a glass, and that glass is like a pearly star. Its oil comes from a blessed olive tree that is neither of the east nor of the west, almost giving off light even if untouched by fire. This is light upon light. Allah guides to His light whom He wills, and He sets forth such parables for mankind.\n\nIn verses 36–38, it is stated that this lamp is found in houses (mosques and masjids) which Allah has allowed to be exalted and in which His name is remembered. Mentioned therein are people who glorify Allah morning and evening, whom neither commerce nor buying and selling distracts from the remembrance of Allah, performing prayer, and giving zakat. These are individuals who fear a Day in which hearts and eyes will be overturned, and whom Allah will reward with the best of recompense; Allah provides without measure to whom He wills.\n\nIn verse 39, the deeds of the disbelievers are likened to a mirage in a desert; a thirsty person thinks it is water, but when he reaches it, he finds nothing, and instead finds Allah there, Who pays him his account in full. In verse 40, their condition is like deep darkness in a vast sea, covered by waves upon waves, over which are clouds. If one stretches out his hand, he can hardly see it. And whoever Allah does not grant light has no light.\n\nIn verses 41–46, it is emphasized that all who are in the heavens and the earth, as well as the birds flying in ranks, glorify Allah, each knowing its prayer; and that the dominion of the heavens and the earth belongs to Allah. Allah drives the clouds, sends down rain, brings down hail from the sky, and causes lightning to flash. He alternates the night and the day. He created every living creature from water; some crawl on their bellies, some walk on two legs, and some walk on four. Allah has sent down clear verses, and He guides whom He wills to the straight path.\n\nOne of the proofs demonstrating Allah's power is that He drives the clouds, brings them together, produces rain from them, sends down hail from the sky striking whom He wills with it, while the flash of its lightning almost takes away the eyesight. Moreover, His alternating of the night and the day, and His creating every living creature from water—bringing some into existence crawling on their bellies, some walking on two legs, and some on four—also point to His power. Allah does whatever He wills; He has power over all things. He has sent down clear verses, and He guides whom He wills to the straight path.\n\nFaith is not merely a matter of verbal utterance; one must believe sincerely from the heart and prove this faith through actions. One of the proofs of faith is to be pleased with and obey the judgment of Allah and His Messenger in the event of a dispute. The hypocrites, on the other hand, come to the judgment of the Messenger of Allah when the truth is in their favor, but turn away when it is against them. According to a narration, a hypocrite named Bishr had a land dispute with a Jewish man; while the Jewish man accepted the Messenger of Allah as an arbitrator, the hypocrite, anticipating an unfavorable outcome, wanted to go to the chief rabbi of the Jews. This incident is an indication of the disease in the hearts of the hypocrites; they are indeed the wrongdoers themselves.\n\nWhen the believers are called to Allah and His Messenger so that he may judge between them, they say, 'We hear and we obey.' These are the ones who attain salvation. Whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger, and fears Allah, it is they who are the truly prosperous. The hypocrites, however, swear by Allah their most solemn oaths, pretending that if you commanded them, they would surely march forth. Yet what is required of them is not false swearing, but sincere obedience. Allah is fully aware of what they do.\n\nObedience to Allah and His Messenger is commanded. Obedience to the Prophet means obedience to Allah. If they turn away, the Prophet's duty is only to convey the message clearly, while your duty is to obey. Allah has promised those who believe and do good deeds that He will grant them succession on the earth just as He granted succession to those before them, establish and protect the religion He has chosen for them, and grant them security after their fear. Establish prayer, give zakat, and obey the Prophet so that you may receive mercy. Those who disbelieve cannot escape Allah; their destination is Hell, and what an evil arrival it is!\n\nO you who believe! Let those whom your right hands possess and those children among you who have not yet reached puberty ask your permission before entering your presence at three times: before the dawn prayer, when you take off your garments at midday, and after the night prayer. These are three times of privacy for you. Outside of these times, they may come and go without permission. When your children reach puberty, let them ask permission at all times, just as the elders ask permission.\n\nThere is no blame upon elderly women past childbearing age who have no hope of marriage if they lay aside their outer garments, provided they do not display their adornments. Yet for them to remain modest—that is, to prefer remaining covered—is better for them. Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing. Furthermore, there is no blame on the disabled, such as the blind, the lame, or the sick; nor is there any blame on you if you eat in your own homes or in the homes of your relatives. When you enter homes, greet one another with peace. Thus does Allah make clear His verses to you so that you may reflect.\n\nThe true believers are only those who genuinely believe in Allah and His Messenger, and when they are with the Prophet on a communal matter, they do not leave without asking his permission. Those who ask his permission are the true believers. The Prophet may grant permission to whomever of them he wills, and he should ask forgiveness for them from Allah. Do not treat the call of the Prophet among you like the call of an ordinary person; let those who oppose his command beware lest a trial strike them or a painful punishment afflict them. Whatever is in the heavens and the earth belongs to Allah; He knows your condition and will inform you of what you did."